1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,111
Somewhere in your city, right now, a
dispatcher is talking to a hundred radios

2
00:00:03,111 --> 00:00:06,223
— on a frequency that didn't exist a
second ago and will be gone a second after

3
00:00:06,223 --> 00:00:09,334
they stop.

4
00:00:09,684 --> 00:00:14,769
Police, fire, transit, utilities —
hundreds of groups, sharing a handful of

5
00:00:14,769 --> 00:00:19,854
channels, coordinated by one data stream
that never stops and never speaks.

6
00:00:20,205 --> 00:00:23,506
Decode that one stream, and the entire
system opens up: every call announces

7
00:00:23,506 --> 00:00:26,807
itself before it starts, and a single
software-defined radio can follow all of

8
00:00:26,807 --> 00:00:30,108
it at once.

9
00:00:30,458 --> 00:00:34,022
This is how trunked radio works — the
pool, the coordinator, the virtual

10
00:00:34,022 --> 00:00:37,587
channels, the grants, and the two ways
calls share the air.

11
00:00:37,707 --> 00:00:41,283
Five ideas, and by the end, you can read a
trunked system like a map.
